  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA cold front will move east through the area later today which \nmay bring a brief window for rain chances this evening and \novernight, mainly across far Northeast Ohio and Northwest \nPennsylvania. Total rainfall amounts are expected to be light \nwith only up to a few hundredths possible. Behind this front, an\narea of high pressure will build south into the region, \nushering in cooler and drier air. Will continue to monitor \ntemperature trends for frost potential Monday night into Tuesday\nwith high pressure directly overhead. However, guidance has \nremained fairly consistent with low temperature magnitude (upper\n30s) which, in conjunction with scattered cloud cover, could \nlimit the overall frost threat. Meanwhile, the frost potential \ncontinues to decrease for Tuesday night into Wednesday morning \nas guidance is favoring a faster onset of warm air advection \nfrom the west. \n\nAnother weak cold front may cross the area by the end of the week, \nthough the latest guidance suggests a mainly dry frontal passage \nwith ample mid-level dry air in place. Gusty southwest winds of 20 \nto 25 mph are possible ahead of the front Wednesday afternoon.",
